WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.561 --> 00:00:18.182
Four days later Sherlock Holmes and I were once again on a train.

2
00:00:18.962 --> 00:00:21.744
This time we were headed to Winchester to see the Wessex Cup race.

3
00:00:22.644 --> 00:00:24.745
Colonel Ross met us at the station in his carriage.

4
00:00:25.565 --> 00:00:28.266
His face was grave and his manner toward us was very cold.

5
00:00:28.286 --> 00:00:31.508
"I've seen nothing of my horse," he said sternly.

6
00:00:32.424 --> 00:00:34.306
"I suppose you'd know Silver Blaze if you saw him?"

7
00:00:34.626 --> 00:00:35.146
said Holmes.

8
00:00:36.027 --> 00:00:37.048
The colonel grew angry.

9
00:00:37.068 --> 00:00:41.912
"A child would recognize Silver Blaze with that distinctive white forehead and his spotted foreleg!"

10
00:00:42.752 --> 00:00:47.016
At the racecourse we took our seats in the grandstand and focused our attention on the horses as

11
00:00:47.056 --> 00:00:48.417
they walked to the starting gate.

12
00:00:49.338 --> 00:00:51.299
Silver Blaze was still listed on the racing card.

13
00:00:51.319 --> 00:00:54.962
"I've put all my hopes on your word, Holmes," the colonel grumbled.

14
00:00:56.363 --> 00:00:57.684
"What colors does your jockey wear?"

15
00:00:58.005 --> 00:00:58.405
I asked.

16
00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:01.728
"A red jacket with a black cap," replied Ross.

17
00:01:02.496 --> 00:01:03.417
"But I don't see him."

18
00:01:04.258 --> 00:01:09.282
"Here he comes," I said as a powerful reddish-brown horse walked by, carrying a jockey in Colonel Ross'

19
00:01:09.322 --> 00:01:11.524
colors. "That's not my horse!"

20
00:01:11.864 --> 00:01:12.404
cried Ross.

21
00:01:12.424 --> 00:01:14.886
"That beast doesn't have a white hair on his body.

22
00:01:15.587 --> 00:01:16.508
What have you done, Holmes?"

23
00:01:16.528 --> 00:01:20.251
"Let's see how that horse does in the race," Holmes said calmly.

24
00:01:21.032 --> 00:01:23.273
He gazed through my binoculars as the race began.

25
00:01:23.294 --> 00:01:25.175
"An excellent start!"

26
00:01:25.495 --> 00:01:26.436
he suddenly exclaimed.

27
00:01:26.456 --> 00:01:28.318
"There they are, coming around the curve!"

28
00:01:29.078 --> 00:01:30.740
From our seats we had a superb view.

29
00:01:31.488 --> 00:01:34.530
The six horses were so close together that a carpet could have covered them.

30
00:01:35.411 --> 00:01:38.273
Holmes spotted the yellow colors of the Mapleton stables jockey.

31
00:01:38.293 --> 00:01:40.734
"Desborough is in the lead!"

32
00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:41.515
he cried.

33
00:01:41.535 --> 00:01:43.176
"But not for long!"

34
00:01:43.956 --> 00:01:47.419
I added as the colonel's horse took the lead and crossed the finish line right in front of

35
00:01:47.499 --> 00:01:49.660
us. "My horse won the race!"

36
00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:50.961
Colonel Ross gasped.

37
00:01:50.981 --> 00:01:53.122
"But I don't understand what's going on.

38
00:01:53.943 --> 00:01:55.264
I think it's time you explained, Mr.

39
00:01:55.284 --> 00:01:58.886
Holmes." "Let's all go look at the horse together," said Holmes.

40
00:01:59.807 --> 00:02:03.405
We went down to a spot on the track, where only owners and their friends were allowed.

41
00:02:04.165 --> 00:02:05.565
A groom led the horse over to us.

42
00:02:05.585 --> 00:02:10.586
"You only have to wash his face and leg to find that he is the same Silver Blaze,"

43
00:02:10.967 --> 00:02:11.507
said Holmes.

44
00:02:11.527 --> 00:02:15.628
"I found him in the hands of someone who had disguised him, and I took the liberty of

45
00:02:15.708 --> 00:02:17.008
running him just as he was."

46
00:02:17.028 --> 00:02:19.669
"My dear sir, you've done wonders!"

47
00:02:20.069 --> 00:02:21.009
exclaimed Colonel Ross.

48
00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:24.970
"The horse has never run better and he looks perfectly healthy.

49
00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:28.351
I owe you a thousand apologies for having doubted your ability."

50
00:02:29.231 --> 00:02:31.032
"Apologies accepted," said Holmes.

51
00:02:31.916 --> 00:02:35.177
"You've done me a great service by recovering my horse," continued the colonel.

52
00:02:35.998 --> 00:02:38.759
"You'd do me an even greater one by finding John Straker's murderer."

53
00:02:38.779 --> 00:02:41.760
"I have already done so," Holmes said quietly.

54
00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:43.481
"He's right here with us."

55
00:02:43.501 --> 00:02:46.422
"What!" Colonel Ross flushed angrily.

56
00:02:46.442 --> 00:02:48.543
"Are you accusing me of murder?"

57
00:02:49.283 --> 00:02:50.024
Holmes laughed.

58
00:02:50.824 --> 00:02:52.985
"The real murderer is standing directly behind you."

59
00:02:53.765 --> 00:02:56.887
He stepped past the colonel and laid his hand upon the horse's glossy neck.

60
00:02:56.907 --> 00:02:58.427
"The horse!"

61
00:02:58.787 --> 00:03:00.168
Colonel Ross and I exclaimed together.

62
00:03:01.032 --> 00:03:02.753
"Yes, the horse," said Holmes.

63
00:03:02.773 --> 00:03:07.456
"I must add, however, that he did it in self-defense and that Straker was not worthy of your

64
00:03:07.496 --> 00:03:12.379
trust." We begged for details, but Holmes wanted to watch the rest of the races.

65
00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.781
He promised to explain everything at the end of the afternoon.

66
00:03:16.661 --> 00:03:20.544
Colonel Ross decided to accompany us back to London because he had some business in the city.

67
00:03:21.424 --> 00:03:26.247
"I arrived at Dartmoor believing that the stranger, Fitzroy Simpson, was the guilty party," said Holmes when we

68
00:03:26.287 --> 00:03:27.188
were settled on our train.

69
00:03:28.044 --> 00:03:31.885
"You may remember that when we arrived at Straker's house, I remained in the carriage for a few

70
00:03:31.925 --> 00:03:36.307
moments. It was just then that the significance of the curried lamb occurred to me."

71
00:03:36.327 --> 00:03:39.908
"Even now I can't see how that helps us," said the colonel.

72
00:03:39.928 --> 00:03:43.549
"Powdered opium has a distinct flavor," said Holmes.

73
00:03:43.569 --> 00:03:46.490
"It's not unpleasant, but it is noticeable.

74
00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:52.051
If it were mixed into an ordinary dish, the diner would undoubtedly taste it and stop eating.

75
00:03:52.771 --> 00:03:54.172
But curry would hide the flavor."

76
00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:58.943
Holmes explained that there was no way that Simpson could have known what the Strakers were serving for

77
00:03:59.003 --> 00:03:59.723
supper that night.

78
00:04:00.664 --> 00:04:02.685
So Simpson was eliminated as a suspect.

79
00:04:03.446 --> 00:04:07.489
The opium was added after the food was set aside for the groom because he was the only

80
00:04:07.609 --> 00:04:08.429
one who was affected.

81
00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:13.773
Since only Straker or his wife could have decided to serve curry, which one added the opium?

82
00:04:13.793 --> 00:04:19.397
"Before answering that question," said Holmes, "I realized the significance of the dog's silence.

83
00:04:20.358 --> 00:04:23.240
The Simpson incident showed me that a dog was kept in the stable.

84
00:04:24.116 --> 00:04:27.457
Although someone had been in and taken the horse, the dog didn't bark.

85
00:04:28.297 --> 00:04:30.398
So obviously the dog knew this person well."

86
00:04:31.878 --> 00:04:35.319
At that point Holmes was fairly sure that Straker went to the stable in the dead of night

87
00:04:35.839 --> 00:04:37.020
and took out Silver Blaze.

88
00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:38.320
But why?

89
00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.261
For a dishonest purpose, or he wouldn't have had to drug his own groom.

90
00:04:43.101 --> 00:04:45.302
Holmes hoped to find the answer in the dead man's pockets.

91
00:04:46.262 --> 00:04:49.143
"And I did—that odd little knife," said Holmes.

92
00:04:49.163 --> 00:04:52.264
"It's possible to nick a horse's tendons and make him lame.

93
00:04:53.136 --> 00:04:57.800
Straker knew that everyone would think the horse had hurt himself while training; no one would suspect foul

94
00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.421
play." "Villain!

95
00:04:59.842 --> 00:05:01.343
Scoundrel!" cried the colonel.

96
00:05:01.363 --> 00:05:06.327
"To accomplish this task, Straker had to take the horse out on the moor," Holmes went on.

97
00:05:06.347 --> 00:05:10.590
"Such a spirited creature would surely rouse the grooms when he felt the prick of the knife."

98
00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.131
"Of course!"

99
00:05:12.552 --> 00:05:13.332
said Colonel Ross.

100
00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:16.015
"That's why Straker needed the match and candle."

101
00:05:16.035 --> 00:05:19.037
"But why would Straker want to hurt the horse?"

102
00:05:19.497 --> 00:05:19.898
I asked.

103
00:05:20.758 --> 00:05:23.333
"So that Silver Blaze would lose the race," said Holmes.

104
00:05:24.273 --> 00:05:26.795
"Straker had probably bet heavily on another horse to win.

105
00:05:27.555 --> 00:05:31.058
And the contents of his pockets also revealed why he needed money so badly."

106
00:05:31.898 --> 00:05:35.101
Holmes pointed out that most men don't carry other men's bills in their pockets.

107
00:05:36.101 --> 00:05:38.123
After seeing the dressmaker's bill and asking Mrs.

108
00:05:38.143 --> 00:05:42.906
Straker about the expensive gray dress, Holmes concluded that Straker was involved with another woman.

109
00:05:43.706 --> 00:05:47.189
When Holmes returned to London, he showed the dressmaker the photograph of Straker.

110
00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:50.011
She immediately identified him as Mr.

111
00:05:50.031 --> 00:05:51.492
Derbyshire—the name on the bill.

112
00:05:52.316 --> 00:05:54.777
On Monday night Straker led the horse out to the hollow.

113
00:05:55.678 --> 00:05:57.418
Simpson had dropped his scarf while fleeing.

114
00:05:58.899 --> 00:06:02.240
Straker picked it up, perhaps intending to use it to secure the horse's leg.

115
00:06:03.021 --> 00:06:04.541
He took off his coat and lit a match.

116
00:06:05.402 --> 00:06:09.884
But the horse, frightened by the sudden light and perhaps sensing something wrong, lashed out.

117
00:06:10.664 --> 00:06:12.725
The trainer was struck in the head by the horse's hoof.

118
00:06:12.745 --> 00:06:16.346
"As Straker fell, his knife gashed his leg," said Holmes.

119
00:06:16.366 --> 00:06:19.848
"I knew what he was up to when the groom told me about the lame sheep.

120
00:06:20.816 --> 00:06:23.677
Straker wouldn't undertake such a delicate job without some practice."

121
00:06:23.697 --> 00:06:26.239
"But where was my horse all this time?"

122
00:06:26.599 --> 00:06:27.219
asked the colonel.

123
00:06:27.999 --> 00:06:30.401
"He bolted and a neighbor took him in," said Holmes.

124
00:06:30.421 --> 00:06:32.522
"I beg you to ask no more."

125
00:06:33.322 --> 00:06:38.164
The great detective winked at me, and I realized that Holmes had promised Silas Brown his freedom in

126
00:06:38.204 --> 00:06:39.865
return for keeping the horse safe.
